event: done
data: {"turn_id":"20b21b3e-1220-4377-a48f-86879137112d","conversation_id":"dbfd9296-9c6b-4a48-aba3-f7e31709abbc","tokens":22181,"tool_calls":4,"revision_attempts":0,"adversarial_concerns":5,"final_text":"The Okta-over-Auth0 identity-provider decision (accepted 2026\u201101\u201130) traces back to a short but clear causal chain:\n\n1. **Field note (2026\u201101\u201128, Marcus Rivera): BlueState data center tour** \u2014 During a 4-hour session with the eligibility-mainframe team, Marcus documented operational constraints (notably the hard 50rps rate cap on their backplain). This on-the-ground context fed into the identity vendor evaluation.\n\n2. **Meeting: \"Identity-provider decision call\" (2026\u201101\u201129, Marcus Rivera)** \u2014 Attendees Lisa Wong, Marcus Rivera, and Tom Reyes (partial) reviewed Marcus's Okta-vs-Auth0 memo. Key input: **Lisa Wong confirmed Okta is already the employee SSO** and tied to the existing BA(A) relationship \u2014 a strong practical/compliance argument for reuse over a net-new vendor (Auth0).\n\n3. **Proposal drafted (2026\u201101\u201129, agent:cartographer)** \u2014 The matrix_extractor agent converted the meeting outcome into a formal decision proposal: \"Identity provider: Okta over Auth0.\"\n\n4. **Proposal accepted (2026\u201101\u201130, Sarah Chen)** \u2014 Sarah Chen approved the proposal, finalizing the decision node.\n\nA parallel, same-day event \u2014 an email from Patricia Vance to Sarah Chen setting up a weekly check-in cadence \u2014 appears in the causal window but looks incidental/administrative rather than a direct driver of this specific decision.\n\n**Resulting graph structure:**\n- The decision node **depends_on** the system node **\"Member Identity (Okta)\"**.\n- Stakeholder **Raj Patel (Compliance)** **sponsors** this decision \u2014 consistent with the compliance/BAA rationale surfaced in the meeting.\n\n**Bottom line:** the decision was driven primarily by reuse logic \u2014 Okta was already the org's employee SSO with an existing BAA in place, which Lisa Wong validated in the decision call, building on operational context Marcus had gathered in the field. Sarah Chen signed off, and Compliance (Raj Patel) is the sponsoring stakeholder on record."}

